### 7. **Complete Data Flow Diagram**

```
Scanned Serial Number
        ↓
Extract Lot Code (e.g., "24H02B.10" from "24H02B.10.0278")
        ↓
Query LotVariation Table (variationNumber = extracted code)
        ↓
    Found?
    ├─ YES → Return variation.canonicalLot (CANONICAL LOT RESOLVED)
    └─ NO  → Query Lot Table (exact match)
            ├─ Found → Return exact Lot
            └─ Not Found → Error: Lot not found
        ↓
Use Canonical Lot ID for:
  • Barrel creation (set lotId)
  • Duplicate detection
  • Lot variation tracking (set lotVariationId if variation)
  • Error logging and resolution
```

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
1. **Variation-First Lookup**: The `findLotByNumberOrVariation` method prioritizes the LotVariation table over exact lot matches, ensuring that if both exist (TEC-1047 scenario), the canonical lot is always selected - this prevents data inconsistency when variation numbers conflict with real lot numbers
2. **Serial Normalization**: The system reconstructs the barrel serial number using the canonical lot number instead of the variation number, so internally all barrels reference the canonical lot - this enables duplicate detection to work correctly even when scanned with variation serials
3. **Cross-Transaction Consistency**: The method intentionally doesn't use transaction clients for lot variation queries (TEC-611), treating variations as semi-static reference data that must be visible regardless of transaction isolation level - this prevents false negatives when multiple operations create barrels concurrently
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

