### **8. Strategy Determination Flowchart**

```
Request arrives with Authorization header
  ↓
MultiStrategyAuthGuard.canActivate() called
  ↓
super.canActivate() delegates to Passport
  ↓
Passport tries strategies in order: ["jwt", "azure-ad"]
  ↓
Strategy 1: JWT Strategy
  ├─ Extract token from Bearer header
  ├─ Verify signature with JWT_SECRET
  ├─ Call userService.user() to find user
  ├─ If success → Return user, authentication complete
  └─ If fail (UnauthorizedException) → Try next strategy
  ↓
Strategy 2: Azure AD Strategy
  ├─ Extract token from Bearer header
  ├─ Fetch signing key from Azure JWKS (v2, fallback v1)
  ├─ Verify token signature
  ├─ Validate issuer against allowed list
  ├─ Validate audience against allowed list
  ├─ Detect token type (delegated vs application)
  ├─ If application token → Create service user
  ├─ If delegated token → Register/update user in database
  ├─ If success → Return user, authentication complete
  └─ If fail (UnauthorizedException) → All strategies failed
  ↓
All strategies failed
  ├─ If development/e2e → Create mock user, return true
  └─ If production → Throw UnauthorizedException
```

---

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
**Passport's strategy chain follows a "fail-forward" pattern**: Each strategy is independent and doesn't know about others. When a strategy throws an exception, Passport interprets it as "this strategy doesn't apply to this request" and tries the next one. This elegant design means both JWT and Azure AD can extract tokens from the same `Authorization: Bearer` header, but they validate differently (shared secret vs JWKS keys). The order matters—JWT is tried first, so if you have both JWT and Azure AD tokens in your system, JWT token holders will authenticate faster. The development fallback at the guard level (not the strategy level) provides a clean way to support offline development without modifying individual strategies.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

