**The partial failure pattern follows three principles**: (1) **Fail-soft semantics** — exceptions in one record don't stop processing of others, using `continue` and try-catch per item instead of throwing; (2) **Full observability** — counts, results arrays, and error arrays all persist to the BulkOperation record, enabling later analysis and recovery; (3) **State mutation independence** — each successful update is committed immediately (no batch rollback), so if processing crashes midway, the next retry only processes unfinished records. This is different from transactional batch processing which would roll back all changes on any failure. The design trades atomicity for resilience, accepting partial success to maximize data consistency in a distributed system where some records may have stale locks or validation failures.
